# ── step 5, the account ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THE FOOTERS ARE THE PRIVACY STORY AND THEY ARE TRUE. This is the only step
# that asks for something about the PERSON rather than the machine, so it is
# the only one where "where does this go?" is a fair question — and the
# answer differs per field, which is exactly why each field says its own:
#
#   name      written to machine.nix on this disk. Nothing leaves.
#   password  hashed the moment it is typed (mkpasswd, stdin — never an
#             argv anyone can read from ps), and the plaintext is never
#             written anywhere, including the answers file.
#   hostname  THE ONE THING THAT LEAVES. DHCP and mDNS announce it to every
#             network the machine joins, so it is the one field where the
#             honest note is a warning rather than a reassurance — and why
#             the default is "golem" rather than anything built from a name.
#
# There is no full-name question. GECOS is cosmetic, it sits in /etc/passwd
# where every local process can read it, and the installer has never needed
# it for anything. One less thing asked is one less thing to keep.
