# ══ THE INVITATION ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# THE ATTRACT LOOP, kept whole from census-mock and now doing more work.
# Until somebody chooses, the invitation cycles through language after
# language on a five-second beat. It is there for the person who cannot
# read the line as it stands: a static English sentence is useless to them
# and, worse, gives no sign it will ever be anything else — the machine
# looks English-only. A line that visibly changes language explains itself
# in a way no single language can. Wait a moment and yours turns up.
#
# Four seconds rather than two because the line is a whole sentence and
# somebody who has just walked up to the machine needs time to finish
# reading it in a language they do not speak before deciding it is not
# theirs.
#
# It stops the instant a language is chosen and never restarts. Motion is a
# request for attention, so it has to stop being one the moment it has been
# answered.
#
# WHAT IS NEW: the rotation is no longer only an invitation, it is a
# PRESELECTION. Press ENTER while your language is the one on screen and
# the filter opens with that language already highlighted — a second ENTER
# takes it. Waiting for your turn is now the fast path, not the patient
# one, which is the difference between a decoration and a control.
#
# ENTER stays the literal word in every line. Census-mock's rule: the key
# cap says ENTER on nearly every keyboard sold, so translating it would
# make the one unambiguous token ambiguous.
INVITE=(
  "en|Press ENTER to select your language"
  "es|Pulsa ENTER para elegir tu idioma"
  "zh-CN|按 ENTER 选择您的语言"
  "hi|Apni bhasha chunne ke liye ENTER dabayein"
  "ar|اضغط ENTER لاختيار لغتك"
  "pt-BR|Pressione ENTER para escolher o seu idioma"
  "fr|Appuyez sur ENTER pour choisir votre langue"
  "ru|Нажмите ENTER, чтобы выбрать язык"
  "de|Drücke ENTER, um deine Sprache zu wählen"
  "ja|ENTER を押して言語を選択"
  "id|Tekan ENTER untuk memilih bahasa Anda"
  "tr|Dilinizi seçmek için ENTER'a basın"
  "it|Premi ENTER per scegliere la tua lingua"
  "ko|ENTER를 눌러 언어를 선택하세요"
  "pl|Naciśnij ENTER, aby wybrać swój język"
  "vi|Nhấn ENTER để chọn ngôn ngữ của bạn"
)

index_of_code() {
  local i
  for (( i = 0; i < NROWS; i++ )); do
    [ "$(f_code "$i")" = "$1" ] && { printf '%d' "$i"; return 0; }
  done
  printf '0'
}

# ══ THE FILTER ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Ranked in two passes, and the order is the whole usability of the step:
# things the query STARTS a word of come first, things it merely appears
# inside come second. So "in" puts Indonesian and Hindi above Finnish,
# where a plain substring search would bury them under every word with an
# "in" in the middle. Within a pass the table's own order is kept, which is
# why the table is ordered by how many people speak the language.
#
# The picker is GENERIC. Both steps that pick from a list — language here,
# keyboard next — load the same four arrays and call the same code, so the
# stranger learns one control once. Copying it per step is how a mockup ends
# up 1238 lines long.
PICK_NAME=()   # what is shown — the whole row
PICK_HAY=()    # lowercased, space-led search text
PICK_NOUN=''   # what the no-match line calls these things
PICK_TITLE=''  # what this step is asking for
PICK_TITLE_HOT=no

# THERE IS NO SECOND COLUMN (Max, 2026-09-05: "we don't need the dimmed side
# of the lists… we want minimalism"). Every list carried one — the language
# code beside English, `us` beside English (US), the local time beside
# America/New_York, the disk model beside /dev/sda — and every one of them
# restated the row it sat next to. Somebody who reads "New York" does not
# need to be told what time it is in New York; they picked it because they
# live there.
#
# What the screen was actually missing is the opposite: it never SAID WHAT
# IT WAS ASKING FOR. Four lists in a row, each one just a caret and some
# names. PICK_TITLE is that line, and it costs the column the asides used to
# waste.

MATCH=()
filter() {
  local q=${1,,} i n=${#PICK_HAY[@]}
  MATCH=()
  if [ -z "$q" ]; then
    for (( i = 0; i < n; i++ )); do MATCH+=("$i"); done
    return
  fi
  q=${q//-/ }
  for (( i = 0; i < n; i++ )); do
    [[ ${PICK_HAY[$i]} == *" $q"* ]] && MATCH+=("$i")
  done
  for (( i = 0; i < n; i++ )); do
    [[ ${PICK_HAY[$i]} == *"$q"* && ${PICK_HAY[$i]} != *" $q"* ]] && MATCH+=("$i")
  done
}

# ══ THE TRANSCRIPT ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# THE VOICE, kept from census-mock. Golem is spoken ABOUT, never as "I" —
# the voice is its maker describing what it did, which stays warm without
# the machine pretending to be a person. Golem acts on the device; WE act
# on Golem, so an unfinished thing is our work and says so. Nothing is ever
# done "for you": saying so out loud is flattery, and the work speaks
# without being pointed at.
#
# An answered question collapses to ONE line and stays. That line is the
# receipt: at the end of the install the whole transcript is on screen and
# the summary screen the TUI needed a whole tile for is just what you
# already read, still there.
IND='  '

# ONE STEP ON SCREEN, AND ONLY THE CURRENT ONE (Max, 2026-09-05). Each step
# opens by clearing what came before it, so what a stranger is looking at is
# always exactly the question being asked. The earlier draft kept a running
# transcript of receipts down the screen; it read like a form being filled
# in, and a form is a thing you scan for what you missed. A question you
# cannot scan past is a question you answer.
#
# What is NOT lost: the current step still shows its own context — step 2
# keeps the layout it is proposing and the census running behind it, because
# those are what the question is ABOUT, not what came before it.
#
# The screen opens with nothing but air. There was a "golem" over the
# invitation and it came off: this is the one screen that cannot assume it
# is understood, and a brand name is a word in a language the reader may not
# have — the exact thing the rotating line exists to avoid. The invitation
# is the first thing on screen because it is the only thing worth reading.
screen() {
  [ "$TTY" = yes ] && printf '%s[2J%s[H' "$esc" "$esc"
  printf '\n'
}

LABELW=13

# Labels are Capitalised HERE, once, rather than in sixty strings across six
# languages — ${1^} lifts the first letter, so "language"→"Language",
# "idioma"→"Idioma", and already-capped tokens (GPU, Wi-Fi) are untouched.
# The value keeps its own case: driver names (i915, iwlwifi) are real module
# names and must stay exactly as the kernel spells them.
#
# $4 is a RED TAIL — text appended in the danger colour after the normal
# value. Only the disk uses it, for "(will be erased)": the drive's name and
# size read plainly, and just the consequence is red.
answered() {                     # $1 label, $2 value, $3 value-colour, $4 red-tail
  local vc=${3:-$C_VALUE}
  printf '%s%s%s%s  %s%s%s' "$IND" "$C_TICK" "$TICK" "$NORM" "$C_LABEL" "$(pad_to "${1^}" "$LABELW")" "$NORM"
  printf '%s%s%s' "$vc" "$2" "$NORM"
  [ -n "${4:-}" ] && printf ' %s%s%s' "$C_RED" "$4" "$NORM"
  printf '\n'
}

# ══ THE STACK ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# AN ITEM IS A SETTLED CHOICE, AND NOTHING ELSE (Max, 2026-09-05). One item
# per finished step, stacked at the top of every screen after it: the
# keyboard step shows one — the language — and the step after it shows two.
#
# The earlier draft used the same row shape for things that were NOT settled
# choices — the layout being proposed, the census running in the background —
# and that is what made the screen unreadable. A row that looks like a
# decision but is not one teaches the reader that the rows mean nothing.
# So the shape is now reserved:
#
#   an item        a decision that has been made and will not be asked again
#   the ask        the decision being made right now — never an item
#   the footer     work happening on its own — never an item
#
# Items survive the per-step clear because they are re-printed from ITEMS,
# not scrolled: the screen is rebuilt each step, so the stack is a fact about
# state rather than about what happens to still be on the terminal.
ITEMS=()
# label|value|aside|colour. The aside is stored-but-unshown (kept for a
# possible future column); the 4th field is the value's colour, which only
# the disk uses — it is the one settled row that names something about to be
# destroyed, so it is the one row that is red.
settle() { ITEMS+=("$1|$2|${3-}|${4-}|${5-}"); }

stack() {
  local it l v a c rt
  for it in "${ITEMS[@]}"; do
    IFS='|' read -r l v a c rt <<<"$it"
    answered "$l" "$v" "$c" "$rt"
  done
  (( ${#ITEMS[@]} )) && printf '\n'
  return 0
}

# ══ KEYS ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
KEY=''
# Returns 0 with KEY set, or 2 on timeout. A TIMEOUT AND A CLOSED STDIN ARE
# NOT THE SAME THING and read reports both as non-zero: >128 for the timeout,
# 1 for end of input. Treating them alike is a spin — every waiting loop here
# repaints and waits again, which on a dead stdin is a busy loop pinning a
# core on a laptop that is meanwhile trying to run the census. There is no
# recovery from stdin going away mid-install, so it leaves here.
# F1, wherever it is offered, abandons the install and drops back to the
# shell. Nothing has been written — the disk is only touched after the
# confirm's ENTER — so cancelling is always safe; the EXIT trap restores
# the cursor, the keymap and the console marker.
cancel_install() { exit 0; }

read_key() {                     # $1 timeout, or empty for none
  local k rest r2 args=(-rsn1) rc
  [ -n "${1:-}" ] && args+=(-t "$1")
  IFS= read "${args[@]}" k; rc=$?
  if (( rc != 0 )); then
    KEY=''
    (( rc > 128 )) && return 2
    show_cursor; printf '\n'; exit 0
  fi
  case $k in
    '')       KEY=ENTER ;;
    $'\e')    IFS= read -rsn2 -t 0.05 rest
              case $rest in
                '[A') KEY=UP ;; '[B') KEY=DOWN ;;
                '[C'|'[D') KEY=SIDE ;;
                # F1 has three forms in the wild: xterm/vt100 sends \eOP,
                # the Linux fbcon console \e[[A, and some terminals \e[11~.
                # The first two chars disambiguate; read the tail only for
                # the two that need it, so arrow keys stay a 2-char match.
                'OP') KEY=F1 ;;
                '[[') IFS= read -rsn1 -t 0.05 r2
                      [ "$r2" = A ] && KEY=F1 || KEY=OTHER ;;
                '[1') IFS= read -rsn2 -t 0.05 r2
                      [ "$r2" = '1~' ] && KEY=F1 || KEY=OTHER ;;
                '')   KEY=ESC ;;
                *)    KEY=OTHER ;;
              esac ;;
    $'\177'|$'\b') KEY=BACK ;;
    $'\t')    KEY=ENTER ;;
    # Ctrl+D — byte 0x04 — is the HIDDEN door to the developer install, read
    # only on the confirm screen and never announced. It replaced Ctrl+Tab,
    # which is impossible: Tab IS Ctrl+I, so Ctrl+Tab sends the same byte as
    # Tab and no terminal (least of all the target's console) can tell them
    # apart. Ctrl+D is a distinct byte on every terminal, and "D for
    # developer" is easier to remember than a combo nobody could type.
    $'\x04')  KEY=DEV ;;
    *)        KEY=$k ;;
  esac
  return 0
}

# ══ STEP 1 — LANGUAGE ════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ROT=0                            # which invitation is on screen
SEED=0                           # the row the filter opens highlighted on
QUERY=''
SEL=0
VIEW=6                           # matches shown at once

paint_invite() {
  local line=${INVITE[$ROT]#*|}
  printf '\r%s[2K%s%s%s%s' "$esc" "$IND" "$C_ASK" "$line" "$NORM"
}

# The filter block is a FIXED height — query, VIEW rows, blank, guide line.
# Fixed because a block that changes height has to erase its own leftovers,
# and every bug in a redrawing terminal UI lives in that erase. Nine lines
# every time, cursor back up nine, done.
#
# The blank above the guide is load-bearing, not breathing room: without it
# the guide reads as one more row of the list, and on a short match set it
# reads as the LAST row — which is the one place a misread costs something,
# because that row is what ENTER would take.
# Title, blank, query, VIEW rows, blank, guide — or two lines shorter when
# the step has no title, which is only ever the language step (a title there
# would have to be written in a language nobody has chosen yet).
pick_block() { if [ -n "$PICK_TITLE" ]; then echo $(( VIEW + 5 )); else echo $(( VIEW + 3 )); fi; }
BLOCK=$(( VIEW + 3 ))

paint_pick() {
  local i idx r
  if [ -n "$PICK_TITLE" ]; then
    # PICK_TITLE_HOT is the disk step and nothing else. The title names the
    # thing ("Select your disk") and then the consequence ("— everything on
    # this disk will be erased"), and ONLY the consequence is red: the label
    # is a normal question, the erase is the one irreversible thing in the
    # whole installer. Splitting on " — " keeps the red on the danger and
    # off the instruction.
    if [ "$PICK_TITLE_HOT" = yes ]; then
      printf '\r%s[2K%s%s%s%s — %s%s%s\n' "$esc" "$IND" \
        "$C_ASK" "${PICK_TITLE%% — *}" "$NORM" \
        "$C_RED" "${PICK_TITLE#* — }" "$NORM"
    else
      printf '\r%s[2K%s%s%s%s\n' "$esc" "$IND" "$C_ASK" "$PICK_TITLE" "$NORM"
    fi
    printf '\r%s[2K\n' "$esc"
  fi
  printf '\r%s[2K%s%s%s%s %s%s%s%s\n' "$esc" "$IND" "$C_ACCENT" "$CARET" "$NORM" \
    "$C_ASK" "$QUERY" "$NORM" "$( [ "$TTY" = yes ] && printf '%s[7m %s[0m' "$esc" "$esc" )"

  for (( i = 0; i < VIEW; i++ )); do
    idx=$(( FIRST + i ))
    printf '\r%s[2K' "$esc"
    if (( idx < ${#MATCH[@]} )); then
      r=${MATCH[$idx]}
      if (( idx == SEL )); then
        printf '%s  %s%s %s%s%s' "$IND" "$C_ACCENT" "$CARET" \
          "$C_ACCENT_TX" "${PICK_NAME[$r]}" "$NORM"
      else
        printf '%s    %s%s%s' "$IND" "$C_VALUE" "${PICK_NAME[$r]}" "$NORM"
      fi
    elif (( ${#MATCH[@]} == 0 && i == 0 )); then
      printf '%s    %sno %s matches "%s"%s' \
        "$IND" "$C_DIM" "$PICK_NOUN" "$QUERY" "$NORM"
    fi
    printf '\n'
  done

  printf '\r%s[2K\n' "$esc"

  # THE GUIDE LINE. census-mock kept one line that is the only place the
  # installer speaks, and it survives the move: a function of state, never a
  # caption. Here it tells you the one thing the screen cannot show, which
  # is what the keys do.
  # With nothing matched, ENTER does nothing — so the guide must not keep
  # saying it takes something. It said so anyway until 2026-09-05, which
  # left anyone who mistyped pressing the one key the screen recommended
  # and watching it do nothing, with no way to know BACKSPACE was the exit.
  # The language step is the only titleless pick (it is the "meet the
  # machine" step — see THE INVITATION), and it is the FIRST page, with
  # nothing to go back TO. There the exit key is "F1 cancel", not the
  # "ESC back" that led nowhere (Max, round 4). Every other pick keeps
  # ESC back — it has a real previous step.
  local exitkey; exitkey=$([ -z "$PICK_TITLE" ] && t key_cancel || t key_back)
  if (( ${#MATCH[@]} == 0 )); then
    printf '\r%s[2K%s%s%s  ·  %s%s\n' "$esc" "$IND" "$C_DIM" \
      "$(t pick_widen)" "$exitkey" "$NORM"
    printf '%s[%dA' "$esc" "$BLOCK"
    return
  fi
  # ESC is on EVERY guide line, because a way back that is not written down
  # is a way back nobody finds. It is last because it is the rarest of the
  # three and the eye reads the line left to right.
  printf '\r%s[2K%s%s%s  ·  %s%s\n' "$esc" "$IND" "$C_DIM" \
    "$( (( ${#MATCH[@]} > VIEW )) \
        && printf '%s  ·  %d %s' "$(t pick_keys)" "$(( ${#MATCH[@]} - VIEW ))" "$(t more)" \
        || t pick_keys )" "$exitkey" "$NORM"
  printf '%s[%dA' "$esc" "$BLOCK"
}

FIRST=0
clamp() {
  (( SEL < 0 )) && SEL=0
  (( SEL >= ${#MATCH[@]} )) && SEL=$(( ${#MATCH[@]} - 1 ))
  (( SEL < 0 )) && SEL=0
  (( SEL < FIRST )) && FIRST=$SEL
  (( SEL >= FIRST + VIEW )) && FIRST=$(( SEL - VIEW + 1 ))
  (( FIRST < 0 )) && FIRST=0
}

# Opening the list on the row the caller nominates is what makes the
# invitation a control rather than an ornament — see THE INVITATION.
pick_seed() {
  local i
  filter "$QUERY"
  SEL=0
  for i in "${!MATCH[@]}"; do
    [ "${MATCH[$i]}" = "$SEED" ] && { SEL=$i; break; }
  done
  FIRST=0; clamp
}

erase_block() {
  local i
  for (( i = 0; i < BLOCK; i++ )); do printf '%s[2K\n' "$esc"; done
  printf '%s[%dA' "$esc" "$BLOCK"
}

# Returns 0 with PICKED set, or 1 if the user backed out of the list without
# choosing (ESC, or BACKSPACE on an empty query). Backing out is not
# cancelling: the caller decides what an empty answer means, and in both
# steps it means "put the question back".
PICKED=-1
pick() {
  PICKED=-1
  # Fixed for the whole pick, so the erase always matches what was drawn.
  BLOCK=$(pick_block)
  pick_seed
  while :; do
    paint_pick
    read_key || continue
    case $KEY in
      ENTER)
        (( ${#MATCH[@]} == 0 )) && continue
        PICKED=${MATCH[$SEL]}; erase_block; return 0 ;;
      UP)    SEL=$(( SEL - 1 )); clamp ;;
      DOWN)  SEL=$(( SEL + 1 )); clamp ;;
      # BACKSPACE only ever erases a character. It used to fall out of the
      # list on an empty query, which meant the key that undoes typing and
      # the key that leaves the step were the same key — so clearing a
      # filter one character too far silently threw the step away.
      BACK)
        [ -n "$QUERY" ] && { QUERY=${QUERY%?}; pick_seed; } ;;
      # ESC is BACK, everywhere in the installer and nowhere else.
      ESC)   erase_block; return 1 ;;
      # F1 abandons the whole install (offered on the language step, which
      # has no "back" to go to; harmless elsewhere).
      F1)    erase_block; cancel_install ;;
      SIDE|OTHER) ;;
      *)     QUERY="$QUERY$KEY"; filter "$QUERY"; SEL=0; FIRST=0; clamp ;;
    esac
  done
}

# ══ THE ORDER THE LIST OPENS IN ══════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Not the table's order, and not alphabetical. When the list opens, the six
# rows on screen are built for the person standing there (Max, 2026-09-05):
#
#   1  English            always, and always first
#   2  the invitation's   what the rotating line was showing when they hit
#      language           ENTER — highlighted, so a second ENTER takes it
#   3  its dialect        if it has one worth distinguishing
#   4  the widest four    Español · Português · Français · Deutsch
#   …  the other fifty
#
# WHY ENGLISH HOLDS THE TOP SLOT even though it is not what the invitation
# was showing: it is the language the rest of this installer falls back to
# when a translation is missing, so it is the one row that must never need
# searching for. It sits at a fixed position for the same reason a keyboard
# has home keys — it never moves, whatever the rotation was doing.
#
# WHY THE PRESET IS SECOND AND NOT FIRST. Position is not what makes it
# takeable; the HIGHLIGHT is. It opens selected, so ENTER takes it from row
# two exactly as fast as from row one, and English gets its fixed slot for
# free. Waiting for your language and pressing ENTER twice is still the
# whole interaction.
#
# WHY THE DIALECT IS THIRD. Somebody who waited for Español may actually
# want Español (Latinoamérica) — that is the single most likely correction
# on this screen, and putting it directly under the thing it corrects makes
# it one ↓ away instead of a search. Same for the two Portugueses and the
# two Chineses. The pairing runs BOTH ways: arrive on es-419 and es is the
# neighbour offered.
PRIORITY=(es pt fr de)
declare -A DIALECT=(
  [es]=es-419  [es-419]=es
  [pt]=pt-BR   [pt-BR]=pt
  [zh-CN]=zh-TW [zh-TW]=zh-CN
)

lang_order() {                   # $1 = the row the invitation was showing
  local seed=$1 i c
  local -A used=()
  local out=()
  _push() { [ -n "${used[$1]-}" ] && return 0; used[$1]=1; out+=("$1"); }

  _push "$EN_ROW"
  _push "$seed"
  c=${DIALECT[${CODE[$seed]}]-}
  [ -n "$c" ] && _push "$(index_of_code "$c")"
  for c in "${PRIORITY[@]}"; do _push "$(index_of_code "$c")"; done
  for (( i = 0; i < NROWS; i++ )); do _push "$i"; done
  printf '%s\n' "${out[@]}"
}

# Load PICK_* from a language order. Both the live step and --dump filter go
# through here so what a dump shows is what a boot shows.
LANG_VIEW=()
lang_view() {                    # $1 = seed row
  local i
  mapfile -t LANG_VIEW < <(lang_order "$1")
  PICK_NAME=(); PICK_HAY=(); PICK_NOUN=$(t l_language); PICK_TITLE=''
  for i in "${LANG_VIEW[@]}"; do
    PICK_NAME+=("${DISPLAY[$i]}"); PICK_HAY+=("${HAY[$i]}")
  done
  SEED=0
  for i in "${!LANG_VIEW[@]}"; do
    [ "${LANG_VIEW[$i]}" = "$1" ] && { SEED=$i; break; }
  done
}

# COMING BACK IS NOT STARTING OVER (Max, 2026-09-06). Esc from the timezone
# must land on the language LIST with the language you chose still selected,
# not on the rotating invitation — the invitation is how a stranger meets
# the machine, and meeting it again is not what "back" means. Every step
# below re-opens on its own last answer for the same reason.
LANG_DONE=no
KB_CHOSEN=''

step_language() {
  local seed_row
  screen
  hide_cursor

  if [ "$LANG_DONE" = yes ]; then
    QUERY=''
    lang_view "$LANG_ROW"
    # Nothing sits behind step 0, so Esc here just re-opens the list.
    while ! pick; do QUERY=''; lang_view "$LANG_ROW"; done
    LANG_ROW=${LANG_VIEW[$PICKED]}
    show_cursor
    UI=${CODE[$LANG_ROW]}
    settle "$(t l_language)" "${DISPLAY[$LANG_ROW]}" "${LOCALE[$LANG_ROW]}"
    return 0
  fi

  while :; do
    # ── the invitation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    QUERY=''
    while :; do
      paint_invite
      if read_key "$SECONDS_PER"; then
        seed_row=$(index_of_code "${INVITE[$ROT]%%|*}")
        case $KEY in
          ENTER|UP|DOWN)   break ;;
          ESC|SIDE|OTHER)  ;;
          q|Q)             printf '\r%s[2K' "$esc"; show_cursor; exit 0 ;;
          BACK)            ;;
          # A printable key is a head start, not a mistake: it opens the
          # list already carrying that letter. Nobody who knows what they
          # want should have to wait out a rotation first.
          *)               QUERY=$KEY; break ;;
        esac
      else
        ROT=$(( (ROT + 1) % ${#INVITE[@]} ))
      fi
    done

    # ── the list ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    printf '\r%s[2K' "$esc"
    lang_view "$seed_row"
    # Backing out hands the screen back to the invitation, which starts
    # again from where it was. Nothing was chosen, so nothing is recorded.
    pick && { LANG_ROW=${LANG_VIEW[$PICKED]}; break; }
  done

  PICK_TITLE_HOT=no
  show_cursor
  LANG_DONE=yes
  UI=${CODE[$LANG_ROW]}          # everything after this line speaks it
  settle "$(t l_language)" "${DISPLAY[$LANG_ROW]}" "${LOCALE[$LANG_ROW]}"
}

# ══ STEP 2 — TIMEZONE ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# THE SAME WORKFLOW AS THE KEYBOARD STEP, on purpose: step 1 proposes an
# answer, the screen shows the EVIDENCE that would prove it wrong, ENTER
# takes it, ↓ opens the list. A stranger learns the shape once and every
# step after it is already familiar.
#
# WHAT THE EVIDENCE IS. You cannot type a timezone the way you can type a
# keyboard, so the confirmable thing is THE CLOCK. "Europe/Madrid — 14:32"
# turns "do you know your IANA zone name" into "is that the time on your
# wrist", which is the only version of the question a stranger can answer —
# exactly the move the echo box makes for the keyboard.
#
# WHY IT IS SECOND, ahead of the keyboard (Max, 2026-09-05). It is the
# cheapest question in the install and it is answered by looking at a clock,
# so it belongs where attention is highest and where the census still has
# work to do behind it. The keyboard step is longer and can absorb the
# remaining census time.
#
# THE ZONE LIST COMES FROM tzdata AT RUNTIME. There are 506 of them and
# they change with the world's politics — Kyiv renamed, Istanbul left DST.
# Carrying a copy would mean keeping it in step; reading /etc/zoneinfo means
# the list is whatever the medium actually has. Only the language→zone
# DEFAULT is ours, and CI proves every one of those names still exists.
TZDIR_CANDIDATES=(/etc/zoneinfo /usr/share/zoneinfo /usr/lib/zoneinfo)
tz_dir() {
  local d
  [ -n "${TZDIR:-}" ] && [ -d "$TZDIR" ] && { printf '%s' "$TZDIR"; return; }
  for d in "${TZDIR_CANDIDATES[@]}"; do
    [ -d "$d" ] && { printf '%s' "$d"; return; }
  done
  return 1
}

